The useful part to keep is the longest palindromic prefix. A two-pointer scan finds a prefix that can be used, and the unmatched suffix is reversed and placed at the front. The shorter prefix is handled by the same method again.

A small dry run for `"aacecaaa"` finds the whole string as a palindrome, so no characters are added. For a string such as `"abcd"`, only `"a"` is kept at the first scan, and the remaining part is processed recursively.

```java
class Solution {
    public String shortestPalindrome(String s) {
        int matched = 0;

        for (int right = s.length() - 1; right >= 0; right--) {
            if (s.charAt(matched) == s.charAt(right)) {
                matched++;
            }
        }

        if (matched == s.length()) {
            return s;
        }

        String suffix = s.substring(matched);
        String smallerResult = shortestPalindrome(s.substring(0, matched));
        String reversedSuffix = new StringBuilder(suffix).reverse().toString();

        return reversedSuffix + smallerResult + suffix;
    }
}
```

The empty string and an already palindromic string both stop without another recursive call. Java creates new strings for the substrings, so this is easier to read than the linear method but can use more memory.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(n^2)` for the temporary substring strings across recursive calls, with `O(n)` recursion depth.

This uses two rolling hash values while scanning from left to right. One hash reads the prefix normally, and the other gives the same prefix its reverse order. When the values are equal, that prefix is a palindrome, so its length is saved.

The remaining suffix is reversed and added before the input. The method is short, but rolling hashes can theoretically have a collision. This version uses one large modulus, which is usually accepted for this problem.

```java
class Solution {
    public String shortestPalindrome(String s) {
        final long base = 29;
        final long mod = 1_000_000_007L;

        long forwardHash = 0;
        long reverseHash = 0;
        long power = 1;
        int longestPrefix = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            long value = s.charAt(i) - 'a' + 1L;

            forwardHash = (forwardHash * base + value) % mod;
            reverseHash = (reverseHash + value * power) % mod;
            power = (power * base) % mod;

            if (forwardHash == reverseHash) {
                longestPrefix = i + 1;
            }
        }

        String suffix = s.substring(longestPrefix);
        String added = new StringBuilder(suffix).reverse().toString();
        return added + s;
    }
}
```

An empty string leaves `longestPrefix` at zero and returns an empty result. The original string is not edited. The hash arithmetic uses `long` so the multiplication does not overflow before taking the modulus.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the suffix and final result.

The longest prefix that is also a palindrome can be found with the KMP prefix table. Build `s + '#' + reverse(s)`. The last prefix-table value gives the length of the palindromic prefix, because a prefix of `s` matching a suffix of its reverse reads the same from both directions.

Dry run: for `s = "abcd"`, the longest palindromic prefix has length `1`. The suffix is `"bcd"`, so its reverse, `"dcb"`, is placed before `s`.

```cpp
class Solution{
  public:
    string shortestPalindrome(string s) {
        string reversed = s;
        reverse(reversed.begin(), reversed.end());

        string combined = s + "#" + reversed;
        vector<int> lps(combined.size(), 0);

        for (int i = 1; i < static_cast<int>(combined.size()); i++) {
            int length = lps[i - 1];

            while (length > 0 && combined[i] != combined[length]) {
                length = lps[length - 1];
            }

            if (combined[i] == combined[length]) {
                length++;
            }

            lps[i] = length;
        }

        int palindromeLength = lps.back();
        string extra = s.substr(palindromeLength);
        reverse(extra.begin(), extra.end());

        return extra + s;
    }
};
```

The separator is important because it prevents a match from crossing between the original string and its reverse. The code only creates new strings and does not modify the input parameter itself.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the combined string and prefix table.
